| The slack server (#1351): one Slack workspace as a Slack app's bot, over Socket Mode (slack-go, no public address). The events `message.received` (filters `channel`, `user`, `thread`, `mentions`; never the text) and `question.answered` (filters `question`, `channel`; the first answer only), each with a `sender` of `slack` and `team/user`, with Slack's name and email, for a channel link (#1762); `list_channels`, and the messaging contract's `list_conversations`, `read_messages` (#4339: newest first, by cursor, the text marked as outside content) and `reply` (#4345: the person's reply from the Messages page, as the bot), `send_message` and `ask_question` (options as buttons, which the answer replaces); no `@channel`, `@here` or `@everyone`; `SLACK_BOT_TOKEN` and `SLACK_APP_TOKEN` | agents | built | Tools, Events | 2026-11-04, against a fake Slack Web API and Socket Mode over a WebSocket (`Go/internal/slackmcp`) | `Go/cmd/slack-mcp/main.go`, `Go/internal/slackmcp` | not yet on a real workspace (`needs-account`, Alex's test, #3477); Agent Loops on the Mac carries it, every Linux host installs it, and the catalogue lists it as `agents/slack` (the two tokens as secrets; the Messaging slot's **Slack…**, #3475); no Connect Slack, no view, no ready-made workflow |
